= Cake

Heat the oven to 325°F (165°C). Grease and flour a 12-cup #Bundt pan{} or a 10-inch tube pan.

Combine the @Duncan Hines yellow cake mix{1%box}(15.25 oz), @instant vanilla pudding mix{1%box}(3.4 oz), @eggs{4}, @cold water{1/2%cup}(118 ml), @vegetable oil{1/2%cup}(118 ml), @pecans{113%g}(1 cup, chopped), and @Southern Comfort{1/2%cup}(118 ml) in a #large bowl{}. Beat with a #mixer{} at medium speed for ~beat{2%minutes}.

> **Box Sizes** — Versions of this kind of cake reworked for today's smaller cake mix add 1/4 cup (31 g) of all-purpose flour to make up the difference.

> **Swaps** — Walnuts work in place of the pecans.

Pour the batter into the pan. Bake for ~bake{1%hour}. Set the pan on a #wire rack{} to cool for ~cool{10%minutes}.

= Glaze

While the cake cools in the pan, melt the @butter{57%g}(4 tbsp) in a #small saucepan{}. Stir in the @water{2%tbsp} and @granulated sugar{99%g}(1/2 cup). Boil for ~boil{3%minutes}, stirring constantly. Remove from the heat and stir in the @&Southern Comfort{1/4%cup}.

Turn the cake out onto a serving plate and prick the top right away with a #skewer{}. Drizzle and brush on half of the glaze with a #pastry brush{}.

Let the cake cool completely. Reheat the rest of the glaze and brush it evenly over the cake.

> **What's Changed** — The boxes are today's 15.25 oz and 3.4 oz sizes. The original calls for an 18 1/2 oz cake mix and a 3 3/4 oz pudding mix. How to cook the glaze and when to brush it on come from Cooks.com's fuller version of the same cake.
